> Reversal and perfect elimination

A perfect-elimination order requires every vertex’s later neighbors to form a
clique. Any graph with such an order is chordal; reversing a Lex-BFS order gives
such an order when the graph is chordal. An arbitrary failed candidate order
alone does not establish nonchordality, but the reversed Lex-BFS candidate has
the relevant equivalence. The checker below explicitly tests later-neighbor
pairs and can return a real vertex and two nonadjacent later-neighbor identities
as a witness. This separate cubic diagnostic is not counted as Lex-BFS work.

The four-cycle fails; adding diagonal0–2 makes the graph chordal. Both orders
are generated by the native Lex-BFS call, reversed explicitly, then checked. The
figure plots actual check outcomes, while the table prints the exact witnesses;
no simulated chordality classifier is introduced.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lex_bfs_peo.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lex_bfs.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <algorithm>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> cycle{{1, 3}, {0, 2}, {1, 3}, {0, 2}}, chorded = cycle;
    chorded[0].push_back(2);
    chorded[2].push_back(0);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original control | actual reversed Lex-BFS is PEO | actual witness vertex, minus1 if " "valid | actual first witness neighbor | actual second witness neighbor | actual checked " "neighbor pairs |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    int k = 0;
    for (const auto &g : {cycle, chorded}) {
        auto o = dm::algorithms::lex_bfs(4, g);
        std::reverse(o.begin(), o.end());
        auto c = dm::algorithms::check_perfect_elimination_order(4, g, o);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
                   k ? "cycle plus diagonal" : "four-cycle", c.valid, c.vertex,
                   c.left, c.right, c.neighbor_pairs);
        x.push_back(k++);
        y.push_back(c.valid ? 1 : 0);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual PEO validity", {37, 99, 235}, 7).x_limits(-.3, 1.3).y_limits(-.2, 1.5).title("Actual reversed-order elimination checks").x_label("control0:cycle; control1:added diagonal").y_label("actual candidate valid:1 yes,0 no").save_svg("build/lex_bfs_peo.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| original control | actual reversed Lex-BFS is PEO | actual witness vertex, minus1 if valid | actual first witness neighbor | actual second witness neighbor | actual checked neighbor pairs |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| four-cycle | false | 1 | 0 | 2 | 1 |
| cycle plus diagonal | true | -1 | -1 | -1 | 2 |
\image(figures/lex_bfs_peo.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual reversed Lex-BFS elimination checks and failure witnesses.)
\alttext(A four-cycle fails the later-neighbor clique test, while its chorded control passes; printed pair counts belong to the separate verifier.)

> The actual complexity of this implementation

The native matrix initializes n^2 cells, then every selection classifies every
remaining vertex exactly once. Thus the actual number of membership tests is

$$\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(n-1-k)=n(n-1)/2.$$
\alttext(The producer performs a triangular quadratic number of actual neighbor tests, even on an edgeless graph.)

Together with complete input-entry scans and dense construction, this
implementation costs O(n^2+E) time and O(n^2+n) runtime storage. Specialized
refinable-set implementations can achieve the paper’s O(n+E), but the old
header’s linear-time claim did not describe these dense scans. Empty graphs
below isolate this distinction: E is zero yet the actual membership count is
quadratic. The plotted matrix-cell and membership counts are selected logical
operations, not CPU instructions, elapsed time or RAM measurements.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lex_bfs_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/lex_bfs.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, cells, tests;
    std::print("| original edgeless vertices | actual dense initialized cells | actual membership tests | " "actual emitted vertices | actual input entries |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n : {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}) {
        auto a =
            dm::algorithms::lex_bfs_analyze(n, std::vector<std::vector<int>>(n));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.work.matrix_cells,
                   a.work.neighbor_tests, a.work.emitted,
                   a.work.adjacency_entries);
        x.push_back(n);
        cells.push_back(a.work.matrix_cells);
        tests.push_back(a.work.neighbor_tests);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, cells, "actual matrix cells", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(
        x, cells, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(x, tests, "actual neighbor tests",
                                             {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(
        x, tests, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4).y_limits(0, 22000).title(
        "Actual quadratic dense work, even without edges").x_label(
        "original edgeless vertex count").y_label("selected actual operation counts").save_svg("build/lex_bfs_work.svg");
}
```
